## Winter is the hardest season for the circulation

Most people think about their heart and blood vessels only once it is too late. Yet there is a fairly reliable calendar to work from: **blood pressure varies with the season**. A 2021 review in the journal *Hypertension Research* summarises the evidence that daytime blood pressure is higher in winter than in summer, that cold temperature is the main reason, and that this wintertime rise is linked to the greater number of cardiovascular events during the cold months ([Narita, Hoshide and Kario, 2021](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41440-021-00732-z)). An earlier review in the same journal adds that other risk factors also run higher in winter, so an individual's estimated cardiovascular risk genuinely differs depending on the time of year ([Modesti et al., 2018](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41440-018-0048-y)).

The practical conclusion follows on its own. A **cardiovascular spa cure** is not undertaken so that you feel well during the week you spend at the spa. It is undertaken so that you enter the heating season, the frosty mornings and the December rush with better conditioning, settled medication and a rehearsed daily routine. Late summer and autumn are the logical window for that — and, as it happens, the calmest part of the spa year.

## What "circulatory diseases" means in the Czech spa system

Czech spa medicine works from a statutory list of indications (Annex 5 to Act No. 48/1997 Coll., set out in detail by Decree No. 2/2015 Coll.). Diseases of the circulatory system form **indication group II**, immediately after oncological conditions. The group covers hypertension, ischaemic heart disease, states after myocardial infarction, states after cardiac surgery, peripheral arterial disease and chronic venous insufficiency.

Mariánské Lázně treats this group. The operator of the town's spa facilities, Léčebné lázně Mariánské Lázně a.s. — today trading as Ensana — lists **diseases of the circulatory system** among its adult indication groups, alongside musculoskeletal, digestive, respiratory, urinary, neurological and skin conditions. It is not a footnote: the combination of natural carbon dioxide, extensive spa forests and a submontane climate is unusually well suited to this diagnosis.

A word on who pays. The Czech indication list governs reimbursement for people insured in the Czech system, where comprehensive spa care covers treatment, accommodation and standard board, and contributory care covers the treatments alone. The indication list groups these diagnoses as follows:

{% indication-picker group="circulatory" /%}

International guests normally arrive as self-payers, or clarify in advance what their own insurer or national scheme will reimburse for planned treatment abroad. The mechanics are set out in our [guide to spa treatment and insurance](/en/magazine/spa-treatment-insurance-guide).

## Carbon dioxide therapies: the reason people come here

Mariánské Lázně sits on volcanic bedrock, and alongside more than forty mineral springs, dry gas rises from it too. **Maria's gas**, from Maria's Spring, has a carbon dioxide content of 99.7 % and occurs at that purity nowhere else.

For the circulatory system, the carbon dioxide bath is what matters. CO₂ is absorbed through the skin, dilates the small vessels and increases peripheral blood flow: in a small crossover study of 18 patients, skin blood flow rose when the legs were immersed in carbon dioxide water but not in plain water ([Hartmann et al., 1997](https://doi.org/10.1177/000331979704800406), *Angiology*; artificially carbonated water, a single bath). That is why carbonated treatments have long belonged to classical balneotherapy for hypertension, peripheral arterial disease and chronic venous insufficiency; an older review summarises the controlled evidence for precisely peripheral arterial disease, microangiopathies and mild hypertension ([Resch and Just, 1994](https://consensus.app/papers/details/624f01a417885d7f9c0caf162686c4cc/), *Wien Med Wochenschr*). In a small study without a control group, 24-hour blood pressure fell in 35 patients with higher baseline readings after three weeks of balneotherapy ([Ekmekcioglu et al., 2000](https://consensus.app/papers/details/39ccd0edaff45a03b6103309fbe833bc/), *Altern Ther Health Med*). We have covered the science separately in [CO₂ baths: what the science says](/en/magazine/co2-baths-science); here it is enough to say that this is a treatment the spa physician prescribes as a series, and whose effect accumulates over weeks rather than days. Carbon dioxide baths are part of the treatment programme at Centrální Lázně, among other houses.

The subcutaneous gas injections drawn from the same source are a separate treatment with a different purpose — they are applied locally, most often around the large joints and the spine, and should not be confused with the cardiovascular cure. What is combined with what is always decided by the spa physician after the initial examination.

## The terrain cure: walking, on prescription

The second pillar of the cardiovascular cure costs nothing and looks like a stroll. The **terrain cure** is supervised walking over graded ground — the physician sets the route, the pace and the ascent according to the patient's capacity, then increases them step by step. In peripheral arterial disease, supervised walking training is very well documented: a Cochrane review of 32 randomised trials with 1,835 participants showed pain-free walking distance lengthened by an average of 82 metres and maximal walking distance by 120 metres compared with usual care, with the effect lasting up to two years ([Lane et al., 2017](https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD000990.pub4), *Cochrane Database Syst Rev*). In ischaemic heart disease, exercise-based rehabilitation reduces the risk of a further infarction and of hospitalisation, according to a Cochrane review of 85 trials ([Dibben et al., 2021](https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD001800.pub4), *Cochrane Database Syst Rev*). In both cases these were outpatient programmes, not studies of the spa terrain cure — but the principle of supervised, gradually increased walking is the same.

Mariánské Lázně has terrain for it that is hard to match. The spa parks run straight into the woods of the Slavkov Forest, and the network of maintained paths begins a few hundred metres behind the colonnade. The Spa Forests educational trail is **5.2 kilometres long, with 23 stops**, and takes an hour and a half to three hours — in character a gentle mountain walk on good forest tracks, which is exactly what the terrain cure needs: ascent, without risk. If you want a fuller picture of the active side of a stay, see our piece on [why movement at the spa extends life](/en/magazine/movement-spa-extends-life).

The place itself contributes. The town lies at roughly 630 metres above sea level and has held the status of a climatic spa since 2023 — the local submontane toning climate is a recognised healing resource, not a marketing adjective. What that means in practice is described on our page about [climate therapy](/en/climate-therapy).

