## What a day of the cure looks like

The rhythm is always similar, and deliberately dull — that is rather the point.

Morning brings the main treatment on the schedule: a carbon dioxide bath, a dry gas wrap or hydrotherapy. Then the rest period lying down, which is not a perk but part of the treatment; after vasodilation the circulation needs time to settle. Blood pressure is measured late morning, and once a week the physician reviews the readings and adjusts the plan — sometimes, in consultation with your own doctor at home, the dosing as well. The terrain cure comes in the afternoon; evenings are quiet.

The drinking cure is not the principal treatment for cardiovascular diagnoses, and it never follows a blanket recommendation. The Mariánské Lázně springs are strongly mineralised and contain sulphates as well as sodium; **the volume, the choice of spring and the timing are set by the spa physician alone**, according to your diagnosis — doubly so with hypertension or heart failure. There is no "drink this many litres a day" here, and it is wise to distrust advice framed that way.

One more thing that spa physicians name as the commonest source of disappointment: the cure does not replace medication. The aim is steadier readings, better exercise tolerance, an ordered routine and less fear of your own body — not coming off your treatment.

## The fourth pillar: the routine you take home

Carbon dioxide treatments and the terrain cure work only for as long as they last. What genuinely remains afterwards is the routine — and here a three-week stay has an advantage that outpatient care cannot offer.

The spa physician sees you weekly and the nurse daily; instead of ten minutes once a quarter, a continuous curve of readings takes shape. On it you can read what the morning chill does to your blood pressure, what the afternoon walk does, and what a few days with a more sensible amount of salt change. The spa diet is not a fast but a demonstration of what such a menu looks like when somebody else prepares it. And exercise is given a fixed place in the day — after three weeks it has become a habit, and habits formed that way survive at home better than the ones you had to invent for yourself.

Part of the stay is also the least photogenic thing about it: an unhurried review of your medication in consultation with your own doctor, the question about smoking and about sleep, and a straight explanation of why results in chronic conditions are counted in months. That is precisely why the cure is repeated for many indications.

## Who the cure is not for

For group II the indication list also names contraindications, and they are worth knowing in advance. They include **second- to third-degree atrioventricular block, heart failure at NYHA class IV and active infective endocarditis**. For repeat treatment, this group additionally assumes nicotine abstinence of more than twelve months. Before an application is made, an ECG no more than two months old is normally required, along with basic laboratory work — cholesterol, triglycerides, blood glucose, urea and creatinine; in hypertensive disease, a fundoscopic examination as well.

All of that is assessed by your own general practitioner or cardiologist, not by the spa. Without current medical documentation, the physician here cannot build the plan that makes the journey worth taking.

## When to come

For stays covered by Czech public health insurance, the length is set by the indication list: for hypertension (II/3) and states after myocardial infarction (II/2) the basic comprehensive care is 28 days, for symptomatic ischaemic heart disease (II/1) contributory care is 21 days, and repeat stays are 21 or 14 days; self-paying guests most often choose two to three weeks, agreed with the spa physician. For chronic diagnoses the cure is repeated — we have described how a classic stay unfolds in our guide to the [classic three-week cure](/en/magazine/classic-three-week-cure-marienbad). If your dates fall in September or October, you are lucky twice over: the town quietens after the summer season, treatments are easier to schedule — and, above all, you finish precisely as the cold begins to tax the circulation in earnest. Further arguments for this time of year are gathered on our page about [autumn in Mariánské Lázně](/en/autumn).

Pack layers, shoes for forest paths, your own blood pressure monitor if you are used to one — and, above all, up-to-date medical reports. The rest is three weeks of regularity, and the fact that here there is finally time for it.
